168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 624 0 R
/E (In an Arduino program, first we define the pins or variables to give names to pin numbers so they are easy to use in the code. For example, we can define a sensor pin to read values.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 625 0 R
/E (Next, the setup\(\) function runs only one time when the Arduino starts. It is used to prepare the pins and start the Serial Monitor so we can see output on the screen.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 626 0 R
/E (After that, the loop\(\) function runs again and again continuously. This allows the Arduino to keep checking inputs and controlling the circuit without stopping.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
171 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 627 0 R
/E (Inside the loop, analogRead\(\) is used to read changing values from sensors like a potentiometer or LDR. These values keep changing instead of just ON or OFF. We can use Serial lines like Serial.println\(\) to display these values in the Serial Monitor so we can understand what is happening.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
172 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (So overall, setup\(\) prepares everything once, and loop\(\) keeps reading sensor values and displaying them continuously.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
